# Cloudflare Pages reads this at deploy time; nothing in the dev server or the
# preview server honors it, so a policy that breaks the app breaks it only in
# production. After changing anything here, load the deployed page and check
# the console for "Refused to …" — that is the only place it is enforced.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()

# Why each directive survives contact with this build:
#
#   script-src 'self'     Vite emits the entry as an external module script and
#                         no inline <script>. Verify with
#                         `grep -c '<script>' dist/index.html` after a build.
#   style-src  'self'     Tailwind ships one external stylesheet. The JSX
#                         `style={{…}}` props are not inline CSS: React assigns
#                         them through the CSSOM, which CSP does not police.
#                         A literal style="" in index.html would need
#                         'unsafe-inline' and is the thing to avoid.
#   font-src   'self'     vite.config.ts sets `assetsInlineLimit: 0`, so every
#                         woff2 is a file. Restoring the default 4 KB limit
#                         base64s one JetBrains Mono subset into the CSS and
#                         this directive then needs `data:` back.
#   img-src 'self' blob:  A document's figures are shown from object URLs
#                         (assets.ts), which are blob:. Without it every
#                         figure in the editor is refused.
#   worker-src 'self'     engine.worker.ts is built to /assets/*.js and started
#                         with `new Worker(new URL(…, import.meta.url))`, and
#                         pdf.js's worker is a same-origin .mjs file started by
#                         pdf.ts: neither is a blob:.
#   frame-ancestors       Replaces X-Frame-Options, which is not repeated here.
#
# What stays shut:
#
#   A PDF must never reach an <embed>, <object> or <iframe>; object-src and
#   frame-src are 'none' to make that failure loud rather than silent. pdf.js
#   runs its plain-JS decoders (`useWasm: false` in pdf.ts) so it needs no
#   'wasm-unsafe-eval'; granting it would be a deliberate widening, not a fix
#   for a console error. Text pulled out of a PDF is attacker-controlled:
#   render it as text nodes. CSP is the second line there, not the first — it
#   cannot save a dangerouslySetInnerHTML.
